{
    "title": "Cotton Board Rules and Regulations: Adjusting Supplemental Assessment on Imports (2025 Amendments)",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 31, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-31/pdf/2025-24113.pdf",
    "summary": "Two federal updates to close out the year: the Agriculture Department is lowering the value used to calculate the extra assessment on imported cotton so it matches what U.S. farmers received in 2024, and the Securities and Exchange Commission is streamlining how it handles requests to keep certain filing details nonpublic.\n\nOn cotton, this is a routine, required adjustment that keeps assessments on imports aligned with those on U.S.-grown cotton. The per\u2011kilogram rate used for imports will tick down, which means importers of cotton and cotton\u2011content goods should see slightly lower bills starting March 1, 2026, unless significant objections are filed by January 30, 2026. If import volumes hold steady, total assessments paid by importers could fall by roughly $3 million. Small and mid\u2011sized importers, brands, and retailers stand to benefit from modest cost relief and predictability, and some organic imports may already be exempt. On the other hand, a lower take from import assessments could trim the Cotton Board\u2019s research and promotion funds, which are intended to support demand for U.S. upland cotton. The change doesn\u2019t alter what U.S. farmers pay; it simply keeps parity. The agency also refreshed the tariff-based table that shows the updated rates product by product.\n\nAt the securities regulator, authority to grant, deny, or revoke requests to keep portions of investment adviser filings nonpublic is being delegated to the director who oversees that program. The aim is faster, more predictable decisions without needing a full Commission vote each time. Investment advisers and fund sponsors may see quicker turnaround and clearer timelines. Some may worry that delegating decisions could reduce direct Commission involvement, but the Commission can still review, and the change is about process, not the standards applied. This update takes effect immediately.\n\nBoth moves are framed as practical housekeeping to promote consistency and efficiency, with modest benefits for filers and importers and limited downsides tied mainly to funding and oversight preferences.",
